// Three vessels on a real sea, and the whole of the scene's arithmetic is that nothing had to be
// trimmed by hand.
//
// **The `vessels` library puts a boat's waterline at `y = 0`** rather than standing it on its keel the
// way every other library here stands its pieces on the ground.  A vessel has no face it rests on; what
// it has is the level it floats at.  So each of these is dropped at the origin and translated about in
// `X` and `Z` only -- no draft looked up, no hull sunk by hand until it looked right -- and the
// [`Water`](#water) surface is laid at the origin to meet them.
//
// **They face `+X`**, like the vehicles and the trains, so a `rotate Y` is the only thing any of them
// needed to point somewhere else.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    antialiasing depth 1
    antialiasing threshold 0.4
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'A Working Morning'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A trawler, a yacht and a submarine, each floating at the level the library says.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

import 'vessels' { Sailboat, Trawler, Submarine, VesselBlue, VesselGreen }
import 'water' { Water, SeaWater }
import 'daylight' { ClearMorning, ClearMorningLight }

camera {
// **Low, because a sea is only bright at a graze.**  From high up you look *into* the water and it
// comes back nearly black whatever the sky is doing -- swapping a morning sky for a noon one moved the
// whole picture by five percent, which is nothing.  What lights a sea is the angle: down at ten metres
// the swells throw the sky back at you and the same water reads twice as bright.
    location [30, 10, -44]
    look at [-2, 2.5, 20]
    field of view 54
}

background ClearMorning
light ClearMorningLight { samples 40 }

// The sea.  A swell rather than a flat plane, because the one thing a boat on glass never looks is
// afloat -- and because the waterline convention is only worth anything if the water it meets is real.
//
// **It has to reach the horizon, and a ray tracer's horizon is at infinity.**  There is no curved
// earth here, so an eye ray only ever becomes parallel to the water -- which means a finite sheet of
// sea always stops short, and below the horizon a physical sky has no colour to give.  Nine hundred
// metres square left an obvious black band under the sky; nine thousand left one black row, at some
// columns, which is the sort of fault that survives every glance and shows in print.  Forty kilometres
// puts the edge three hundredths of a degree under the horizon and the row goes away.
object Water(40000, 40000, 'choppy') { material SeaWater }

// The yacht, close and on the starboard tack.
object Sailboat(10) { rotate Y 22  translate [-4, 0, -12] }

// The trawler, working, and turned across the picture so her bulwarks and derrick read.
object Trawler(22) { rotate Y -34  translate [10, 0, 28] }

// And a boat on the surface, a long way off, which is the only place a submarine ever looks large.
object Submarine(84) { rotate Y 146  translate [-96, 0, 150] }
